kamla harris has accepted the democratic  party's nomination  for the post of vice president of the  united states  it is the first time a woman of black  and indian american heritage  has been nominated for such a position  by any major u.s  political party besides the u.s her  candidature is also generating  excitement  in the country of her mother's birth  india  a nation where kamala still has family  ties  but the question really is if and how  that will impact relations between the  us and india  panes of kamala harris not in the united  states  but in india the possibility of an  indian american becoming the next u.s  vice president  is stoking national pride especially in  her grandparents native chennai  in the south of the country it gives me  immense pride as a woman to learn that a  woman with roots in this city is all set  for the u.s vice presidential election  if you see it's the second generation  right  and if you see how wonderfully she has  brought up the two girls  they are so very well educated and yet  rooted  they have not forgotten where they  belong  kamala harris invoked her indian  heritage while accepting her nomination  for the u.s vice presidency  and there's another woman whose name  isn't known  whose story isn't shared another woman  whose shoulders i stand on and that's my  mother  shamala gopalan harris  she came here from india at age 19  to pursue her dream of curing cancer  many indians wonder what a winning biden  harris ticket in november would mean for  relations with the united states  she should win the election and she  should also be in favor of  india what she does to the us and  how the american diplomacy with india  will be or will exist after she became  the vice president that's all matters  a sentiment many in india will see as  the true test of kamala harris's indian  heritage  let's get more on this druva joshanker  is director of the u.s initiative of the  indian think tank  the observer research foundation and he  joins me now from  washington dc driver great to have you  on the program  just straight off the bat would it make  any difference to u.s  india ties if kamala harris were to be  elected vice president  of the united states  i mean i think the short answer is  actually no uh that she's running for  vice president of the united states not  of india  um she will be you know i think as a us  leader and she's shown this as a senator  and in other positions during her  political career  that that you know she will be it's  natural that she'll be looking out  uh for the best interests of uh the  united states in its uh foreign  relations  um and uh so so i mean i think that  that's really  at the the key uh in some ways her story  you know i mean it's quite it's quite  natural that people uh  in india will see uh in her story a sort  of a story of inspiration and and be  proud of  of what she and her family have achieved  in um  but ultimately it will have as much of  an impact on u.s india relations as  barack obama's presidency had for  u.s kenya relations which is not very  much uh  that's an interesting analogy then why  does kamala harris keep bringing up her  indian heritage for example in her  speeches  well it is a remarkable story her mother  coming uh  as a very young student to the united  states by herself  and meeting you know kamala harris's  father  uh raising two children as a single  mother being becoming you know getting a  phd  it is quite a remarkable life story uh  so it's it's interesting you know it is  it makes sense for her to to to hearken  up uh back up  upon it um but the other thing is i  think there is definitely an attempt to  appeal to  indian american voters and people you  know uh  for women uh people who you know will  find inspiration in kamla harris's  heritage and background so i think that  a big reason why even in her speech last  night where she  uh you know she used the tamil word uh  the the indian language word for uh for  her aunts  uh something like that will resonate  very positively with a lot of indian  american voters speaking of the indian  american vote  uh dhruva is it important only to get  candidates elected or does it  also somehow impact relations between  india and the u.s  um there is some relation uh of course i  mean for a lot of indian-american voters  um the relation good relations between  the united states and india  does matter it is one of the issues uh  on their policy agenda that they would  like to see  um to see materialized and and so so i  in that sense we did see for example on  saturday on august 15th which is india's  independence day both  joe biden and kamala harris making a  statement about  uh two to the indian american community  on india's independence day um so  there is that that connection but  ultimately i think this is this is  really about  a minority community in the in the  united states um and they have a set of  uh issues one of only one of which is  relations with  with india that resonate in that matter  quite a lot for them i'd like to talk to  you about the indian government surely  there would be a preference potentially  in the current indian government  dealing either with a biden white house  or a trump white house  which do you think they prefer and why  uh you know i don't think there's a  strong preference either way i think  each  uh candidacy and each platform uh offers  certain  advantages uh from an indian point of  view um  and certain disadvantages as well so for  example with uh you know  trump has taken uh competition with  china quite seriously and sometimes to  the benefit of the u.s india's strategic  relationship uh  in some ways india has been a major  beneficiary of trump's free and open  indo-pacific policies  uh at the same time i think that there  are deep and abiding concerns about  trump's  approach to trade his approach to  immigration uh and  those two things in particular uh and uh  i expect if  should he be re-elected that he would  take a much stronger position especially  on on immigration which will have  uh some negative impact for india uh on  the other hand you know the  administration i think will uh be much  you know  much more open to partnering with india  on issues like trade and climate change  particularly so the  the latter being something that has been  off the agenda um  and uh you know we'll offer some relief  from an indian point of view on  immigration  but there are concerns about how  seriously they will take the  geopolitical  approach to to china and and to pakistan  to to issues that india has  serious concerns about so so i think  each candidacy offers certain  upsides and downsides from an indian  perspective we leave it there for the  time being but thank you so much for  joining us druva jaishankar speaking to  us from washington dc
 

